<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="51559" href="schedule/2011/spring/GLBL/296/51559.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2011" href="schedule/2011.xml">2011</calendarYear><term id="120111" href="schedule/2011/spring.xml">Spring 2011</term><subject id="GLBL" href="schedule/2011/spring/GLBL.xml">Global Studies</subject><course id="296" href="schedule/2011/spring/GLBL/296.xml">Global St Foundation Seminar</course></parents><sectionNumber>EM </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Critical Human Rights in Glbl</sectionTitle><sectionDateRange>Meets 18-Jan-11 - 11-Mar-11.</sectionDateRange><creditHours>1 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Critical Human Rights in Global Perspective. Human rights are not "natural," but historically located ideas through which political claims are made. In this course we explore a range of "rights," from children's rights to rights to health. We ask: in the post-Cold War, post-9/11, globalizing environment, how have ideas about human rights transformed, deformed, or reformed? This course will open to non Global Studies majors on Monday, November 29th, 2010.</sectionText><partOfTerm>A</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2011-01-18Z</startDate><endDate>2011-03-11Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>03:00 PM</start><end>04:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>T      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>331</roomNumber><buildingName>Armory</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Moodie" firstName="E">Moodie, E</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>